WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:07.760
Let's now get into some details on these data&nbsp;
types. I'm back in the sales.salesorderheader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.240
table, and we want to see why these data&nbsp;
types have been chosen for these columns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.840
Integer columns can have numbers ranging from&nbsp;
approximately negative to positive two billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:25.520
Small integer numbers range&nbsp;
from -32767 to positive 32767.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.560
Tiny integers range from zero to 255. If&nbsp;
you know a number range will be smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.880
you may want to use a smaller number value&nbsp;
to use fewer bytes overall for storage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:45.280
For money fields, the number range is about&nbsp;
negative 922 to positive 922 trillion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:52.960
which, of course, is huge. Smallmoney values range&nbsp;
from negative to positive 214000. For text fields,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.240
we covered elsewhere the number of bytes&nbsp;
varchar fields use versus nvarchar fields.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.640
That said, nvarchar fields allow for Unicode&nbsp;
characters while varchar fields do not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.760
Two columns in this table show this well. This&nbsp;
CreditCardApprovalCode column uses varchar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.120
There is no need for symbols in this column.&nbsp;
Let's contrast that with the Comment column,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:23.280
which is an nvarchar column. Someone posting&nbsp;
comments may want to include Unicode symbols,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.320
hence the need for nvarchar even though nvarchar&nbsp;
columns use almost double the space of varchar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:40.160
columns. For date and time fields, the datetime&nbsp;
field has a date range of 1753 to 9999 in years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.800
and time can be calculated to the thousandth of a&nbsp;
second. Just to compare, the smalldatetime field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:54.240
has a date range of 1900 to 2079, specifically&nbsp;
June 6, 2079. More importantly, time can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.720
broken down to the second but no further. A bit&nbsp;
field, shown here with the OnlineOrderFlag field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:08.240
is a 0 or 1 field and is usually used as a Boolean&nbsp;
field, otherwise known as a Yes/No field or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:13.840
True/False field. To recap, the type of data you&nbsp;
need to store in a column and the range of data or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.920
the characters of data stored in a column dictates&nbsp;
the data type you will use. You will want to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.600
sure you choose a data type that stores the data&nbsp;
you need while using as few bytes as possible.

